Submodel V2 (constraints (1)-(13g): stability + centre of mass + load bearing), n = 18-22.
150 runs: 5 sizes x 10 instances x 3 solvers. Objective (1), lower is better.

MILP (Gurobi) and BS (beam search) run under a 3s time limit; both are V2-feasible by construction
and were verified: 0 violations of either the CoM pyramid or load bearing across all runs.

WFBF is NOT time limited (single deterministic pass; runtime measured, not budgeted) AND, more
importantly, it is NOT a V2 method: it has no CoM or load-bearing logic. Its packings were checked
against V2 anyway and FAIL on 7 of the 50 instances (2 CoM pyramid violations, 5 load bearing),
spread over every size. Its column is therefore a REFERENCE POINT, not a valid V2 result, and its
objective is optimistic - it is not paying for constraints the other two must satisfy.

WFBF below also runs in "corner" mode, not the paper's literal Algorithm 1 (every beta-grid
position; beta=1 on these instances makes that intractable per-run). See
Results_WFBF_grid_vs_corner_n18-22 for the direct grid-vs-corner comparison: corner mode is both
faster and gives a better objective here, so only the WFBF TIME column should be treated as an
approximation artifact, not the objective.

All methods see the same derived weights, CoMs and load limits (seed=1, alpha=0.8, Lambda=3x).
MILP is 0/10 optimal at every size: it reaches TIME_LIMIT with ~100% gap, i.e. the bound has not
moved off zero in 3s, so the gap measures the missing bound, not the quality of the incumbent.

